{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "pl-03-unit-rates-quantity-deals",
  "version": 1,
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Unit Rates, Quantity Deals, and Buying or Selling by Count",
    "description": "Compare prices quoted per item, dozen, kilogram, metre, or bundle by converting to a common quantity.",
    "teachingApproach": "Develop the commercial relationship step by step, explain why each rate uses its base, show intermediate amounts, check signs and units, then practice transfer with misconception-specific retries.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 50,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 12 “Profit and Loss,” printed pages 374–425. Approved scope: Solve profit/loss problems with rates such as x items for a price, per-dozen prices, bundles, weight, and length. Choose a convenient common quantity (often an LCM), compute total CP and SP on that quantity, and then find the percentage on CP. Include reciprocal rate reasoning when asked how many units to buy or sell for a fixed amount. Preserve units through the calculation and check that a higher unit receipt corresponds to a gain. Use solved examples 11–15 and matching objective patterns."
  },
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Unit Rates, Quantity Deals, and Buying or Selling by Count: Compare prices quoted per item, dozen, kilogram, metre, or bundle by converting to a common quantity. We establish the model, explain each step, and practice with targeted feedback.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A rate such as “6 for ₹10” combines a price and a quantity. Convert to cost per item, or scale both purchase and sale to a common count before deciding profit or loss.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_2"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_2": {
      "id": "teach_2",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Suppose items are bought 6 for ₹10 and sold 4 for ₹6. The buy rate is ₹10/6 each and sale rate is ₹6/4=₹1.50 each. Because ₹1.50 is below ₹1.666…, this is a loss.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_3"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_3": {
      "id": "teach_3",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Using a common count avoids recurring decimals. The least common multiple of 6 and 4 is 12: purchase cost ₹20, receipts ₹18, loss ₹2; loss rate=2/20=10%.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_4"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_4": {
      "id": "teach_4",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For fixed-price bundles, compare the price of equal quantities rather than comparing the two bundle counts directly. A larger number per rupee means the buyer’s unit price is lower.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_5"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_5": {
      "id": "teach_5",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If 10 units cost ₹8 and each sells for ₹1.25, revenue is ₹12.50. Profit is ₹4.50 on ₹8 cost, so gain is 56.25%, not 45%—the percentage divides by cost.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_6"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_6": {
      "id": "teach_6",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "“How many for a rupee?” asks for a quantity rate. If 6 buttons cost ₹1 and a 20% gain is desired, the same 6 buttons must bring ₹1.20. At ₹1, the seller can supply only 5 buttons.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_7"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_7": {
      "id": "teach_7",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "When quantities include dozen, kilograms, metres, or pairs, first convert to the same unit. One dozen is 12 pieces; a per-dozen rate cannot be compared directly with a per-item rate.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_8"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_8": {
      "id": "teach_8",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A common multiple can also solve totals across different pack sizes. Find a count divisible by both quantities, calculate the total CP and SP for that count, then take the net difference.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_9"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_9": {
      "id": "teach_9",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If asked for profit per unit, divide total profit by the number of units. If asked for percentage profit, divide by total CP. Those are different outputs.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_10"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_10": {
      "id": "teach_10",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Check that the direction makes sense: buying fewer items for the same money raises unit cost; selling fewer items for the same money raises unit receipt.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_11"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_11": {
      "id": "teach_11",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For fractional quantities or repeating rates, keep fractions until the final step. For example, ₹10/6 is exactly ₹5/3, so comparing with ₹3/2 is straightforward.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_12"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_12": {
      "id": "teach_12",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Avoid mixing bulk prices and retail prices. If the seller buys 40 goods at the listed price of 36 but sells all 40 at 1% off list, compare 39.6 of list receipt with 36 of list cost.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_13"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_13": {
      "id": "teach_13",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "That example produces profit ₹3.60 on cost ₹36, hence 10%. A discount from marked price does not mean loss unless the actual receipt falls below CP.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_14"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_14": {
      "id": "teach_14",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For each rate problem, write “cost for a standard count” and “receipt for the same count.” This makes the denominator for profit percentage visible.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_15"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_15": {
      "id": "teach_15",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Finish with a unit check: currency per item times item count returns currency; a ratio of quantities is unit-free. A mismatched answer often reveals an incomplete conversion.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "focus_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "focus_1": {
      "id": "focus_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Name the cost, receipt, and percentage base. Choose an operation that follows the transaction wording.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
